    <p class="eyebrow">Trust &amp; Standards</p>
    <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>

    <h2>Purpose and scope</h2>
    <p>Food Supply Crisis Report covers food availability, prices, agricultural inputs, climate and water pressures, logistics and food security. We aim to explain what a source establishes, what remains uncertain and how a finding applies to a particular commodity, country or population. A local shortage, a price rise and acute humanitarian hunger are different conditions and should not be described as interchangeable.</p>

    <h2>Source hierarchy</h2>
    <p>For important factual claims, we prioritize the original data or report from organizations such as FAO, WFP, IPC, national statistical offices, agriculture ministries, official weather agencies and peer-reviewed research. Secondary reporting can provide context or point us to an event, but it is identified as reporting when an original official document or dataset cannot be located. A source link is not presented as proof of a claim it does not directly measure.</p>
    <p>We check the date, geographic coverage, unit, comparison period and revision status of quantitative claims. A global commodity index should not be described as a national grocery price index; a forecast should not be described as a confirmed outcome; and a report about one place should not be generalized to an entire country without supporting evidence.</p>

    <h2>Evidence labels and uncertainty</h2>
    <p>Articles distinguish recorded observations, official estimates, forecasts and editorial analysis through the wording used. Forecasts can change as new data arrives. When evidence is incomplete or conflicting, the article should explain the limitation instead of choosing the more dramatic interpretation.</p>

    <h2>Authorship and research tools</h2>
    <p>Articles carry the “Food Supply Crisis Report Editorial Desk” byline, which is a publication byline rather than a claim of individual professional credentials. Research, drafting and editing tools—including AI-assisted tools—may be used in preparing material. Tool output is not itself an authoritative source; factual claims should be checked against the linked source material, and readers are encouraged to consult those originals. The publication does not claim specialist credentials that are not listed.</p>

    <h2>Publication dates, updates and corrections</h2>
    <p>The publication date indicates when an article first appeared. An updated date should be used when a material change is made, such as a corrected statistic, a meaningful new source or a revised interpretation. Cosmetic edits alone do not justify presenting an article as newly reported. If a source changes its estimate, the article should identify the newer release and avoid leaving conflicting figures unexplained.</p>
    <p>Readers can report an error through the <a href="/contact/">Contact</a> page. Please include the article URL, the specific statement, why it may be wrong or incomplete, and a reliable source supporting the correction. We prioritize material factual corrections and aim to make important changes clear in the article.</p>

    <h2>Independence and advertising</h2>
    <p>Advertising is separate from editorial decisions. Advertisers do not determine article conclusions, source selection or the interpretation of data. We do not ask readers to click advertisements, and we do not use an advertisement as evidence for a factual claim.</p>

    <h2>Reader-first publishing</h2>
    <p>Pages should answer a real reader question with context, evidence and useful links. We aim to avoid unsupported alarm, duplicated pages, inflated claims, repetitive filler and headlines that promise more certainty than the source provides. When a page is not useful without a future update, it should be improved or retired rather than repeatedly republished for search visibility.</p>

    <p>For the source directory, see <a href="/sources/">Sources &amp; Data Providers</a>. For the publication's purpose and limits, see <a href="/about/">About</a>.</p>
